# CL1 — Spiritual Sense

Spiritual Sense is always active unless suppressed.

## Range

| CL | Range |
|---:|---:|
| 1–10 | 10 feet |
| 11–16 | 30 feet |
| 17–20 | 60 feet |

Within range, you perceive active souls, spirits, possessing entities, projected souls, spiritually animated corpses, and creatures with ordinary living consciousness even when normal sight cannot perceive them.

Against an ordinary living creature, Complete Soul, or spirit within range, Spiritual Sense functions as Blindsight. It does not automatically perceive:

- soulless traps or objects;
- mundane writing, color, or visual detail;
- a construct with no mind, spirit, or animating soul;
- a corpse with no active spirit or necromantic animation;
- a creature behind total cover;
- a successfully concealed soul, Secret concealment, divine veil, artifact-level masking, sealed spirit, or stronger spiritual absence.

Spiritual Sense also tells you whether a perceived entity is broadly:

- Embodied, Manifested, Hosted, possessing, projected, Unbodied, Anchored, or Contained;
- Resonance Marked by you;
- under an obvious ordinary possession, coercive Hosting, or spirit-manifestation effect.

It does not reveal exact statistics, thoughts, memories, Soul Anchor, Spirit Nature, Subpath, full Bond terms, or hidden Source Rules.

## Read the Spirit

**Action Type:** Action  
**Range:** Your Spiritual Sense range

Choose one soul, spirit, possession link, spiritual residue, Soul Fragment, or spiritual effect you perceive. Learn:

- its broad category;
- whether its Source CL is lower than, equal to, or higher than yours;
- whether it is stable, strained, damaged, concealed, Hosted, bound, possessing, Anchored, or suppressed;
- whether it is a Complete Soul, Soul Fragment, Spiritual Imprint, or uncertain mixture.

For a concealed or protected effect, make a Cultivation Check against its save DC. If it has no save DC, use `8 + source Proficiency Bonus + source key ability modifier`.

On failure, you know that spiritual concealment or interference is present, but not its exact nature. You cannot retry until the effect changes, you obtain a new Source Rule or evidence, or a new scene begins.

---

